{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EX6wbN8zhGs14GM5S4WH5F","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary:\n\n**Score: 4/6 (Weak Accept) | Confidence: 4/5**\n\n**Key strengths identified:**\n- Thorough ablation structure with the label-only loss decomposition (26% gradient dilution / 74% information overload) being a genuine analytical contribution\n- Honest handling of the ae_only fallback contamination confound\n- Multiple generalization axes (cross-model, cross-task, seed robustness, trace length)\n- Appropriate statistical methodology (bootstrap CIs, McNemar's test)\n\n**Major weaknesses identified:**\n1. **Exception dominance undermines the three-component narrative** -- exception_only alone accounts for ~83% of VOTA's gain; loop_only's +2.2pp may not be significant; the \"super-additive synergy\" claim is likely a ceiling-effect artifact\n2. **Dataset provenance is undocumented** -- no information on source repos, split strategy, or whether train/test share projects\n3. **99.2% accuracy not fully explained** -- residual leakage or task triviality not adequately ruled out; trace diversity analysis is missing\n4. **Cross-task (DebugBench) adaptation is unspecified** -- making the generalization claim non-reproducible\n5. **Gradient dilution decomposition overclaims precision** -- assumes additivity of non-independent factors\n6. **Internal inconsistencies** -- exception_only reported as both 95.1% and 89.0%; VOTA traces described as both ~85 and ~250 tokens; model may be Qwen2.5-Coder not CodeLlama\n\nThe review is saved at `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_4/projects/trace-verdict-study/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T084908/best/review_report.md`."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a9d8e823685840c95 (use SendMessage with to: 'a9d8e823685840c95' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 69956\ntool_uses: 21\nduration_ms: 147705</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"92dde915-20d3-4721-be88-dcd90139ca96","uuid":"5cb46543-bc70-4002-95cf-6f9dd02e9cba","timestamp":"2026-05-15T09:30:33.433Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Paper: \"Verdict-Optimized Trace Abstraction: Decision-Relevant Trace Compression for Test Outcome Prediction\"\n\n### Summary\nVOTA is a compact trace representation for binary verdict tasks (pass/fail prediction). It extracts 3 components from raw execution traces: (1) exception/error summaries, (2) loop compression markers, (3) assertion structure. Resulting traces average ~85 tokens vs ~1800 for full traces (21x compression). On test outcome prediction (n=1,190), VOTA achieves 99.2% accuracy vs no-trace 75.1% and full-trace 81.8%.\n\nKey finding: exception summarization is the critical component, contributing ~83% of VOTA's gain (exception_only: 95.1%). Loop-only provides only +2.2pp over no_trace (not statistically significant). A trivial error-presence rule achieves 89%.\n\n### Paper Contents (key tables and sections)\n\n**Table 1: Component Ablation (n=1,190)**\n| Condition | Acc | 95% CI | Δ no_trace | Δ VOTA | Pass F1 | Fail F1 |\n|-----------|-----|--------|------------|--------|---------|---------|\n| VOTA (full) | 99.16% | [98.6, 99.7] | +24.0pp | — | 0.994 | 0.986 |\n| exception_only | 95.1% | [93.8, 96.4] | +20.0pp | −4.1pp | 0.968 | 0.909 |\n| ae_only† | 84.7% | [82.6, 86.8] | +9.6pp | −14.5pp | 0.898 | 0.729 |\n| loop_only | 77.3% | [74.9, 79.7] | +2.2pp | −21.9pp | 0.835 | 0.632 |\n| no_trace | 75.1% | [72.8, 77.5] | — | −24.0pp | 0.813 | 0.627 |\n| Trivial error rule | ~89% | — | +13.9pp | −10.2pp | — | — |\n†ae_only affected by fallback contamination\n\n**Table 2: Trivial Rule vs SFT**\n| Method | Accuracy | Beyond-Rule Gain |\n|--------|----------|-----------------|\n| Trivial error rule | 89.0% | — |\n| exception_only SFT | 95.1% | +6.1pp |\n| VOTA SFT | 99.2% | +10.2pp |\n| full_trace SFT | 81.8% | −7.2pp |\n\n**Table 3: Cross-model (DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B)**\n| Condition | Primary | DeepSeek | Δ |\n|-----------|---------|----------|---|\n| VOTA | 99.2% | 97.1% | −2.1pp |\n| full_trace | 81.8% | 79.3% | −2.5pp |\n| no_trace | 75.1% | 72.8% | −2.3pp |\n| VOTA lift | +24.0pp | +24.3pp | +0.3pp |\n\n**Table 4: Cross-task (DebugBench bug detection, n=800)**\n| Condition | Test Verdict | Bug Detection | Δ |\n|-----------|-------------|---------------|---|\n| VOTA | 99.2% | 89.7% | −9.5pp |\n| full_trace | 81.8% | 74.1% | −7.7pp |\n| no_trace | 75.1% | 67.3% | −7.8pp |\n| VOTA lift | +24.0pp | +22.4pp | −1.6pp |\n\n**Table 5: Trace Length Ablation**\n| Trace Length | Accuracy | Tokens | Acc/Token |\n|-------------|----------|--------|-----------|\n| VOTA | 99.2% | ~85 | 1.17%/tok |\n| 256 tokens | 84.3% | 256 | 0.33%/tok |\n| 512 tokens | 83.6% | 512 | 0.16%/tok |\n| 1024 tokens | 82.4% | 1024 | 0.08%/tok |\n| 2048 tokens | 81.1% | 2048 | 0.04%/tok |\n| Full (variable) | 81.8% | ~1800 | 0.05%/tok |\n\n**Table 6: Calibration**\n| Condition | Accuracy | ECE↓ | Brier↓ | Overconf % |\n|-----------|----------|------|--------|------------|\n| VOTA | 99.2% | 0.012 | 0.008 | 2% |\n| full_trace+label-only | 86.2% | 0.058 | 0.105 | 12% |\n| full_trace | 81.8% | 0.085 | 0.142 | 20% |\n| no_trace | 75.1% | 0.148 | 0.195 | 35% |\n\n**Table 7: Seed Robustness (3 seeds)**\nMean 99.13 ± 0.21%, max spread 0.42pp, no McNemar significance.\n\n**Training:** Standard SFT with cross-entropy loss over all tokens. Input: [code] c [test] t [trace] τ [verdict]. Label-only loss variant also tested.\n\n**Discussion claims:** (1) Exception dominance as design principle. (2) Compression beats completeness due to gradient dilution (~26%) + information overload (~74%). (3) Trivial pattern defense: full_trace also has error keywords but scores below trivial rule.\n\n**Limitations acknowledged:** 2 verdict tasks, Python only, ae_only confounded, n=1190 eval set.\n\n### Experimental Design Reference (imagined experiments from plan)\nAll imagined experiments appear covered in the paper:\n- plan_003: component ablation (exception_only, loop_only, ae_only, trivial_rule) ✓\n- plan_004: DeepSeek-Coder cross-model ✓\n- plan_005: DebugBench cross-task ✓\n- plan_006: trace length ablation ✓\n- plan_007: calibration analysis ✓\n- plan_008: seed robustness ✓\n\n### Your Review Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the task definition clean? Is the VOTA extraction pipeline well-defined? Are training procedures appropriate?\n2. **Experimental design quality**: Are ablations comprehensive? Are comparisons fair? Are baselines appropriate?\n3. **Statistical rigor**: Are confidence intervals, significance tests, and sample sizes adequate?\n4. **Result interpretation**: Are claims supported by the data? Are effect sizes meaningful? Any overclaiming?\n5. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to replicate?\n6. **Weaknesses in experimental methodology**: Missing controls, confounds, threats to validity?\n\nProvide your review in this format:\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods & Experiments\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n- S3: ...\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n- W3: ...\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: ...\n- Q2: ...\n\n### Minor Issues\n- ...\n\n### Score: X/6\n### Confidence: X/5\n```\n","agentId":"a9d8e823685840c95","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary:\n\n**Score: 4/6 (Weak Accept) | Confidence: 4/5**\n\n**Key strengths identified:**\n- Thorough ablation structure with the label-only loss decomposition (26% gradient dilution / 74% information overload) being a genuine analytical contribution\n- Honest handling of the ae_only fallback contamination confound\n- Multiple generalization axes (cross-model, cross-task, seed robustness, trace length)\n- Appropriate statistical methodology (bootstrap CIs, McNemar's test)\n\n**Major weaknesses identified:**\n1. **Exception dominance undermines the three-component narrative** -- exception_only alone accounts for ~83% of VOTA's gain; loop_only's +2.2pp may not be significant; the \"super-additive synergy\" claim is likely a ceiling-effect artifact\n2. **Dataset provenance is undocumented** -- no information on source repos, split strategy, or whether train/test share projects\n3. **99.2% accuracy not fully explained** -- residual leakage or task triviality not adequately ruled out; trace diversity analysis is missing\n4. **Cross-task (DebugBench) adaptation is unspecified** -- making the generalization claim non-reproducible\n5. **Gradient dilution decomposition overclaims precision** -- assumes additivity of non-independent factors\n6. **Internal inconsistencies** -- exception_only reported as both 95.1% and 89.0%; VOTA traces described as both ~85 and ~250 tokens; model may be Qwen2.5-Coder not CodeLlama\n\nThe review is saved at `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_4/projects/trace-verdict-study/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T084908/best/review_report.md`."}],"totalDurationMs":147705,"totalTokens":69956,"totalToolUseCount":21,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3307,"cache_read_input_tokens":66209,"output_tokens":439,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3307},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":439,"cache_read_input_tokens":66209,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3307,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3307,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":5,"searchCount":1,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":15}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a2327b157f801a58f","tool_use_id":"toolu_014jFeVpqD4HKUojyTkTcMW7","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work Review","usage":{"total_tokens":52334,"tool_uses":19,"duration_ms":136407},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"b36338a1-068e-4d16-a5b1-bf1cb3c56f1f","session_id":"92dde915-20d3-4721-be88-dcd90139ca96"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01JpeAG9qpZ8Z9TVueHwZ5md","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Jgh2xRYnXXjNrzoonPVqTb","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\nThe paper introduces VOTA, a task-specific trace compression scheme for binary verdict prediction (pass/fail) that retains exception summaries, loop compression, and assertion snapshots. VOTA-SFT achieves 99.2% accuracy versus 75.1% (no-trace) and 81.8% (full-trace), with ablations decomposing the full-trace gap into information overload (74%) and gradient dilution (26%). The core thesis is that trace granularity should match task output entropy, and that verdict tasks benefit from aggressive, decision-relevant compression rather than faithful execution replay.\n\n### Strengths\n\n- S1: **The task-dependency thesis is well-argued and genuinely useful.** The paper's central framing -- that trace SFT value depends on the match between trace granularity and task output entropy -- is a clean, testable claim that reframes prior negative results (DCSH's <3pp on generation) as task-specific rather than universal. This is a real intellectual contribution that could redirect the trace-SFT research agenda.\n\n- S2: **The causal decomposition is methodologically rigorous.** The label-only loss ablation (Section 5.2) cleanly separates gradient dilution (26%) from information overload (74%), which is a level of mechanistic analysis rarely seen in empirical NLP papers. The rule-based ceiling analysis (Section 5.5) is particularly clever: showing that full-trace SFT (81.8%) matches hand-crafted rules (82.0%) provides strong evidence that full-trace models learn only surface features.\n\n- S3: **The VOTA-no-ae ablation (99.4%) is the paper's strongest single result.** It definitively eliminates the most threatening trivial explanation (actual==expected string matching) and forces the reader to engage with the behavioral-understanding interpretation. This was clearly the right experiment to run first.\n\n- S4: **Super-additive synergy analysis (+4.3pp) adds depth.** Demonstrating that exception and loop components are complementary, not redundant, elevates VOTA from \"just concatenated features\" to a principled multi-signal design. The error analysis of where synergy arises (loop-drift failures vs. single-call exceptions) is convincing.\n\n- S5: **Cross-model replication with remarkably consistent lift magnitudes.** The +24.0pp lift on both CodeLlama and DeepSeek-Coder is striking and suggests the finding is robust to model architecture differences.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- W1: **The novelty of \"compress traces for classification\" is debatable.** The idea that binary classification tasks need less information than generation tasks is arguably intuitive -- it follows directly from information-theoretic first principles. The paper acknowledges this framing (\"O(1) output entropy\") but does not sufficiently address whether any practitioner would have done otherwise. The specific VOTA design (exception + loop + assertion) is reasonable feature engineering, but the \"why\" insight (match trace granularity to task entropy) is closer to a design heuristic than a research contribution. A stronger novelty claim would require either (a) a formal framework for trace-task matching, or (b) evidence that the community was actively getting this wrong.\n\n- W2: **Related work coverage is thin for a top venue.** The paper cites only ~5 references in the Related Work section. Critical missing works include:\n  - **NExT (ICML 2024)**: Self-training on execution trace CoT rationales for program repair -- directly relevant as trace-augmented training for a non-generation task.\n  - **Code Execution as Grounded Supervision (EMNLP 2025)**: Uses Snoop debugger traces as natural language CoT, showing cross-domain transfer -- relevant to the \"what kind of trace information helps\" question.\n  - **ET-CoT / PyTracify (ICML 2025 Workshop)**: Compact structured traces for SFT, achieving strong results -- a direct methodological precursor to VOTA's compression approach.\n  - **TraceCoder (ICSE 2026)** and **TraceRepair (2026)**: Both demonstrate that selective/probe-based trace collection outperforms full traces -- the same conclusion as VOTA but in repair settings.\n  - **APPT (TSE 2024)**: Fine-tuned models for patch correctness binary classification (79.7% accuracy) -- the closest existing work to VOTA's task formulation.\n  - **Ferrando et al. (2025)**: Compact scratchpad >= regular scratchpad, directly supporting the \"value redundancy\" finding.\n  The paper's own literature map identifies 16 relevant papers but the draft cites only a fraction. For EMNLP, the related work needs to be comprehensive and show clear differentiation from each related line.\n\n- W3: **Exception dominance undermines the multi-component design narrative.** Exception-only achieves 89.0% (+13.9pp), loop-only achieves 81.0% (+5.9pp), and the \"super-additive synergy\" of +4.3pp brings VOTA to 99.2%. But the exception component alone captures ~58% of VOTA's total lift (13.9/24.0), and the trivial has_ERROR rule gets 79%. This raises the question: is VOTA's real contribution just \"include exception summaries in the prompt\"? The paper tries to address this with the no-ERROR subset analysis (exception-only still 85% on samples without [ERROR] markers), but this needs more emphasis and a clearer articulation of what the model learns beyond error presence.\n\n- W4: **The task may be too easy to support general conclusions.** A 71.5% majority baseline with 99.2% VOTA accuracy on n=1,190 samples raises ceiling-effect concerns. The DebugBench cross-task result (91% VOTA) helps, but the paper draws broad conclusions (\"trace representations should be designed for the downstream task's output entropy\") from what could be a dataset-specific phenomenon. The paper needs to discuss what properties of this specific benchmark make it amenable to near-perfect prediction and whether those properties are representative of real-world verdict tasks.\n\n- W5: **Some reported results appear projected rather than measured.** The paper reports cross-model (DeepSeek-Coder), cross-task (DebugBench), seed robustness (3 seeds), trace length ablation, and calibration analysis as completed results. However, based on experiment tracking, several of these experiments appear to still be in \"planned\" status (plan_004 through plan_008), and the suspiciously round numbers in some tables (e.g., DebugBench: 68.0%, 74.0%, 91.0%; trace length: 78.0%, 82.0%, 83.0%, 82.0%) suggest these may be projections rather than actual measurements. If these are extrapolated, this is a serious integrity concern that must be addressed before submission. If they are real, the CIs and exact values should be reported with the same rigor as the main results.\n\n- W6: **The \"information granularity matches task entropy\" principle lacks formal grounding.** This is presented as the paper's key insight, but it remains an informal heuristic. The paper provides one data point on each side (traces help generation little per DCSH; traces help classification a lot per VOTA), which is insufficient to establish a general principle. What about medium-entropy tasks (e.g., test case generation, partial program completion)? Without a more systematic treatment of the entropy spectrum, the \"principle\" is really just an observation about two points.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n- Q1: Several results (cross-model, cross-task, seed robustness, trace length ablation, calibration) show suspiciously round numbers and lack the detailed CIs present in the main results. Can you confirm these are from completed experiments and provide the raw eval_results.json / bootstrap_compare.json files?\n\n- Q2: The exception-only model (89%) combined with the has_ERROR rule (79%) suggests that a large fraction of the task reduces to \"did an error occur and what kind.\" What is the accuracy of exception-only on the subset of samples where the test passes but with warnings or edge-case behavior? This would better isolate whether the model learns genuine behavioral reasoning versus error-type classification.\n\n- Q3: How sensitive is VOTA's design to the specific Python testing framework? The assertion snapshots and exception formats are Python-specific. Would the same three-component decomposition work for, say, Java JUnit or JavaScript Jest? This matters for the generality of the \"design principles.\"\n\n- Q4: The paper claims VOTA teaches \"execution-grounded verdict reasoning.\" Can you provide attention analysis or probing experiments that demonstrate the model attends to semantically meaningful trace components rather than surface-level structural patterns (presence/absence of [ERROR], [ASSERT], [LOOP] tokens)?\n\n- Q5: Given the literature map's 16 papers, why does the Related Work section cite only ~5? Several omitted works (NExT, ET-CoT, APPT, TraceCoder) are directly relevant and published at top venues.\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n- The primary model is listed as \"CodeLlama-7B\" in the paper but project files reference \"Qwen2.5-Coder-7B-Instruct.\" This discrepancy needs clarification.\n- Table 1 is missing Pass F1 and Fail F1 values (shown as \"---\") for most conditions despite these being available in the results data.\n- The paper uses \"~250 tokens\" for VOTA length but the project summary says \"~85 tokens.\" This inconsistency should be reconciled.\n- The instruction_length citation in Section 2 appears to be a placeholder -- no actual reference is provided.\n- The \"information overload\" terminology is imprecise. \"Attention dilution\" or \"signal-to-noise degradation\" would be more mechanistically grounded.\n\n### Score: 4/6\n### Confidence: 4/5\n\n**Score justification:** The paper addresses a real gap (trace SFT for classification), provides thorough ablations, and the causal decomposition (gradient dilution vs. information overload) is methodologically strong. However, the novelty bar for a top venue main conference is not clearly met: the core insight (less information can be better for simple tasks) is intuitive, the related work is inadequately covered given the rich existing literature, and several reported results may be projections rather than completed experiments. The exception-dominance finding and the near-perfect accuracy on what may be an \"easy\" task weaken the claimed generality. A score of 4 (Weak Accept) reflects solid empirical work with meaningful analysis that falls short of the novelty and completeness expected at the top tier of EMNLP Main, but would be appropriate for Findings.","project":"trace-verdict-study","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260515T084908","overall_score":"4/6 — Weak Accept (Findings-level)"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":685,"cache_read_input_tokens":51073,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":685,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_014jFeVpqD4HKUojyTkTcMW7","session_id":"92dde915-20d3-4721-be88-dcd90139ca96","uuid":"4f5ee540-7257-4570-834e-548833ef5172"}
